																		The real horror was navigating social situations the whole time
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2902864&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608238129" alt="MaliceAforethought" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2902864)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/maliceaforethought" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2902864); return false;" >MaliceAforethought</a></span> <span class="odate time_1582418440 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 Feb 2020 00:40</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4512859">
									

<p>This is a weird one, even for me. It's essentially an attempt to make an article where the narrative is wholly in the containment procedures; I did a similar thing with <strong><a href="/scp-5790">SCP-5790</a></strong> &#8212; <strong>[DATA KILLED]</strong>, and funnily enough this was <em>also</em> a potential contribution for the SCP-5000 contest. In the end, I think my choice made sense &#8212; there's really not enough of a mystery theme here to justify it.</p>
<p>Image source is in the caption.</p>
<p><strong>EDIT:</strong> For those of you disappointed by the text not changing: I feel you. I had the same idea. I just didn't feel like the added expense of writing the whole thing to be javascript compatible would pay off that much story-wise; I <em>also</em> don't like the idea that people would upvote simply based on the effort of a gimmick. This feels like a much more concept-oriented piece, rather than just a flex of my programming abilities. Plus, it's a well-known fact that well-constructed fake randomness always feels more random than actual randomness :P</p>

							</div>

								
